  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Drier Friday, though some scattered showers remain \npossible in the afternoon.\n\nThe low responsible for yesterday's rain is shifting off to the \nnortheast and is just offshore of Cape Cod as of the time of \nwriting. Its associated trough will shift NE as well into Maritime \nCanada, allowing ridging aloft to make its way into the northeast. \nOnshore winds will continue through this afternoon, lending to a \ncontinued chance for some scattered showers over much of eastern MA \ninto RI. Parts of western CT may also see some scattered showers \nthrough the evening. Continued moisture aloft and onshore flow will \nkeep cloudier conditions in place into tonight as well, ushering in \nhigh pressure and more NW flow that will aid in clearing the region. \nHigh pressure returning will lend to the possibility of late \nmorning/early afternoon seabreezes as well Friday as the \naforementioned NW flow remains light. However, the chance for some \nscattered showers lingers in the afternoon. 925 mb temperatures \naround 18C will support highs making a return to the upper 70s and \nlow 80s after being under a cooler airmass these last few days.\n\nLight to calm SW winds Friday night in conjunction with clearer \nskies may increase the chances for radiational cooling. Given the \nrecent rainfall, this all may increase chances for patchy fog \ndevelopment overnight into Saturday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Slow moving frontal boundary focuses soaking \nrain from showers and thunderstorms late Sunday night into \nMonday, perhaps lingering into early Tuesday along the southeast\ncoast. \n\nA slow-moving cold front working through NY/PA Sunday brings our \nnext chance for rain for late Sunday evening into at least much of \nMonday. The main change in the past 24-hours worth of guidance is \nthat the frontal boundary has slowed down about 12 hours compared to \nprevious, in response to stronger meridional flow nearly parallel to \nthe frontal boundary. That also leads to a influx of slightly higher \nprecipitable water values which in some global model solutions are \nupwards of 2\". Main risk this front will bring to Southern New \nEngland is another period of soaking downpours given the slow-moving \nfrontal zone interacting with the highly moisture-rich airmass \npreceding it. It's still uncertain what the highest rain totals \nwould look like and where they would fall, but with a few spot areas \nin interior Southern New England having registered quite a bit of \nrain in the last 48 hours, there would be some risk for localized \nstreet flooding but still view this risk as a lower chance. EPS/GEFS \nprobabilities still show moderately high (40-60%) probs 24-hr rains \nover a half inch but low (< 30%) probs of 24-hr rains over an inch. \nThis is something that will need to be monitored in later guidance. \nparticularly if the frontal boundary slows down more. It now looks \nlike the front won't sufficiently clear Southern New England until \nlater Monday, and could still linger into Tuesday across southeast \nNew England. It still doesn't look like thunderstorms will be \ncapable of severe weather with a weakly-unstable, weak-lapse-rate \nand weak-shear convective setting. Southerly flow also increases \nover the waters, which could prompt marine headlines for our \nboaters/mariners.",
